// Printorian — the half of the reboot guard that is safe to ship without a host.
//
//     sudo install -m 0644 deploy/apt/99printorian-unattended-upgrades \
//       /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/99printorian-unattended-upgrades
//
// `unattended-upgrades` is what keeps the farm host's kernel and openssl patched
// without a person, and `deploy/readiness-check.sh` already warns when it is
// missing. Its default is to install the patch and, if the update needs one,
// reboot — which on this box means killing whatever plate is on a bed at the time.
// A twelve-hour print stopped at hour eleven is spent filament, a spent bed and an
// order that has to be remade.
//
// So the automatic reboot is switched off here. This file makes an unattended
// reboot strictly LESS likely and can never cause one, which is why it ships now
// while the other half — a timer that asks `deploy/reboot-guard.sh` and then
// reboots when the answer is "quiet" — deliberately does not. That half needs a
// Debian host and a real print to prove, and an unproven `systemctl reboot` in
// this repository is the exact failure the guard exists to prevent
// (deploy/systemd/README.md, "What is deliberately not here").
//
// The cost of leaving it here: patches that need a reboot are installed and then
// wait, so the host runs a patched-on-disk kernel it is not yet running. That is
// visible as `/var/run/reboot-required` and is a thing a person decides about,
// which is the right trade while nothing on the box can tell a print from an idle
// evening on its own.
Unattended-Upgrade::Automatic-Reboot "false";
